  "text": "Since $P$, $Q$, and $R$ are the midpoints of sides $AB$, $BC$, and $CA$ respectively, the three circles with diameters $AP$, $BQ$, and $CR$ intersect at corresponding points $X$, $Y$, and $Z$.\n\nLet's denote the lengths of the segments connecting the midpoints to the vertices of the triangle as follows:\n\n$AP(x_1)$, $BQ(x_2)$, and $CR(x_3)$.\n\nSince $P$, $Q$, and $R$ are midpoints, we know the following relationships:\n\n$AB = 2x_1$, $BC = 2x_2$, $CA = 2x_3$.\n\nSubstituting the values of the sides of the triangle:\n\n$13 = 2x_1$, $14 = 2x_2$, $15 = 2x_3$.\n\nFrom the above equations, we get:\n\n$x_1 = \\frac{13}{2}$, $x_2 = \\frac{14}{2}$, $x_3 = \\frac{15}{2}$.\n\nNow, we need to find the area of $\\triangle XYZ$. Since the circles intersect at corresponding points $X$, $Y$, and $Z$, they are concurrent at $P$, $Q$, and $R$.\n\nTo find the area of $\\triangle XYZ$, we can use the fact that the points $X$, $Y$, and $Z$ form a triangle with the vertices of the triangle $ABC$. We know that the lengths of the sides of $\\triangle XYZ$ are half the lengths of the sides of $\\triangle ABC$. Therefore, we can find the area of $\\triangle XYZ$ by finding the area of $\\triangle ABC$ and then dividing it by 4.\n\nLet's find the area of $\\triangle ABC$. We can use Heron's formula:\n\n$s = \\frac{AB + BC + CA}{2} = \\frac{13 + 14 + 15}{2} = 21$.\n\n$Area_{ABC} = \\sqrt{s(s-AB)(s-BC)(s-CA)}$\n\n$Area_{ABC} = \\sqrt{21(21-13)(21-14)(21-15)}$\n\n$Area_{ABC} = \\sqrt{21 \\times 8 \\times 7 \\times 6}$\n\n$Area_{ABC} = \\sqrt{7056}$\n\n$Area_{ABC} = 84$\n\nNow, we find the area of $\\triangle XYZ$ by dividing $Area_{ABC}$ by 4.\n\n$Area_{XYZ} = \\frac{1}{4} \\times Area_{ABC}$\n\n$Area_{XYZ} = \\frac{1}{4} \\times 84$\n\n$Area_{XYZ} = 21$\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle XYZ$ is $\\boxed{21}$.",
